This is a classic "dashboard-with-saved-views" architectural pattern. When building this without heavy state machines like Redux or TanStack Query, the key is to leverage React Context for localized feature state combined with controlled/uncontrolled state boundaries to prevent the entire page from re-rendering when a user interacts with a single dropdown.
Here is your architectural blueprint for a highly performant, reusable, and UX-optimized solution.
________________________________________
1. UI/UX Structure & Flow Recommendations
To ensure a seamless user experience, we need to solve a few common friction points with view management:
•	The "Dirty State" Indicator: When a user modifies filters or column orders on an existing view, change the view dropdown label to * View Name (Modified). This gives immediate visual feedback.
•	The Action Bar Placement: Place the Save and Revert buttons immediately next to the View Dropdown in Row 1, or anchored to the top-right of the entire filter bar. Keeping them contextual to the "View" concept prevents layout shifting.
•	Footer Navigation: In the view dropdown, style the footer sticky rows distinctly (e.g., a subtle top border, slightly different background) so they don’t blend into the selectable list.
•	The "Apply" vs. "Instant" Filter Dilemma: Because you have an explicit Apply button, the filter dropdowns should hold local transient state and only commit to the global page state when "Apply" is clicked. This prevents the table from refetching/re-rendering on every single checkbox click.
________________________________________
2. Component Breakdown (Maximizing Reusability)
To ensure these components can be shared across the app, we decouple the layout from the data fetching.
Row 1: View Management
•	<ViewSelector />: The main dropdown component.
o	<DropdownMenu /> (Generic): Reusable dropdown wrapper.
o	<ViewOptionItem />: Individual view list items.
o	<DropdownFooterLink />: Reusable component for the "Create" and "Manage" action rows.
•	<ViewActions />: Renders the Save Changes and Revert buttons conditionally based on isDirty state.
Row 2: Filter Controls
•	<FilterBar />: Flex/Grid wrapper that handles responsiveness.
o	<FilterDropdown /> (Generic): A reusable multi-select/single-select popover. Must maintain internal open/close and temporary checked states.
o	<Button /> (Generic): Primary for Apply, Secondary/Text for Reset.
Row 3: Data Presentation
•	<DataTable />: A wrapper around material-react-table. It accepts columns, data, visibility state, and order state as props, keeping it entirely presentational.
________________________________________
3. State Management Strategy
Without Redux or TanStack Query, the best approach is a localized React Context Pattern acting as a mini-state machine for this specific page/dashboard feature.
State Categorization
We split state into Committed State (what the table and view engine care about) and Transient State (what the user is playing with before hitting "Apply").
State Piece	Type	Location	Purpose
savedViews	Array	Context	List of all user views fetched from API.
activeViewId	String/ID	Context	The currently active view configuration.
committedFilters	Object	Context	Filters currently applied to the data query.
transientFilters	Object	Local <FilterBar />	Unsaved filter selections (pre-Apply).
columnVisibility	Object	Context	Controlled MRT visibility state.
columnOrder	Array	Context	Controlled MRT column order state.
isLoading	Boolean	Context	Global table skeleton/spinner state.
The "Is Dirty" Matrix
To show Save and Revert, use a derived state selector inside your Context provider:
JavaScript
const isDirty = 
  JSON.stringify(committedFilters) !== JSON.stringify(activeView?.filters) ||
  JSON.stringify(columnVisibility) !== JSON.stringify(activeView?.visibility) ||
  JSON.stringify(columnOrder) !== JSON.stringify(activeView?.order);
________________________________________
4. Performance Optimization (Avoiding Re-renders)
material-react-table can be heavy if the parent component re-renders blindly. Here is how you keep interactions at 60fps:
A. Isolate Transient State
Do not update the global Page Context every time a user checks a box in a filter dropdown.
•	Keep the selections in the <FilterBar /> local state or individual <FilterDropdown /> states.
•	Only lift it up to the Context (as committedFilters) when the Apply button is clicked.
B. Memoize Table Props
material-react-table relies heavily on object references. If you pass raw objects, it will re-render infinitely.
TypeScript
// Inside your Table Component container
const memoizedColumns = useMemo(() => columns, [columns]);
const memoizedData = useMemo(() => data, [data]);

// Use useCallback for MRT state change handlers
const handleColumnVisibilityChange = useCallback((updater) => {
  setColumnVisibility(updater);
}, []);
C. Context Splitting (Optional but Highly Recommended)
If clicking a filter dropdown causes the table rows to lag, split your Context into a StateContext and a DispatchContext. This ensures components that just trigger actions (like buttons) don't re-render when the state values change.
________________________________________
