Here is how our localized state machine (the DashboardProvider context) gracefully coordinates these events.
By separating Transient State (uncommitted UI changes) from Committed State (what the table and API actually read), we keep performance snappy and prevent unnecessary re-renders.
________________________________________
1. User changes the view selected
•	The Flow: The user selects a different saved view from the <ViewSelector /> dropdown.
•	State Handling: 1. The activeViewId is updated to the new view's ID. 2. The Context state clears out any current filter changes and overwrites committedFilters, columnVisibility, and columnOrder with the raw, saved configuration objects of the newly selected view. 3. The local transientFilters in the <FilterBar /> are synchronized to match this new view. 4. The isDirty flag automatically recalculates to false (since the current state now exactly matches the newly active view). 5. The table's loading state (isLoading) is set to true while the new dataset fetches based on these fresh filters.
2. Manage View popup: the user deletes the view already selected
•	The Flow: The user opens the management modal and deletes the view they are currently actively looking at.
•	State Handling:
1.	Upon API deletion success, the view is removed from the global savedViews array in the context.
2.	The context detects that the deleted view ID matches the activeViewId.
3.	The activeViewId resets to a fallback state (either a system default view, an "All Data" empty view, or null).
4.	The table resets its columns and filters back to the factory defaults associated with that fallback view.
3. New filters are applied, and the user clicks on the "Apply" button
•	The Flow: The user checks some boxes inside a <FilterDropdown /> and hits Apply.
•	State Handling:
1.	While interacting inside the dropdown, only the local transientFilters state changes. The table does not re-render or refetch.
2.	When Apply is clicked, the transientFilters value is copied over into the context's committedFilters.
3.	The isDirty derived state evaluates to true because committedFilters no longer matches the filters object stored inside the activeView.
4.	The Save Changes and Revert buttons instantly appear.
5.	The data fetching hook detects the change in committedFilters and triggers the API request to update the table data.
4. Click on the "Save Changes" button
•	The Flow: The user likes their modified configuration and wants to overwrite the current saved view.
•	State Handling:
1.	A payload containing { id: activeViewId, filters: committedFilters, visibility: columnVisibility, order: columnOrder } is sent to your backend API via a PUT/POST request.
2.	Upon a successful 200 OK response, the context updates that specific view's object inside the savedViews array.
3.	The isDirty flag instantly recalculates to false because the current table/filter layout now perfectly mirrors the newly updated database record.
4.	The Save Changes and Revert buttons safely transition out of sight.
5. Click on the "Revert" button
•	The Flow: The user wants to abandon all their modifications and return to the view's original state.
•	State Handling:
1.	The context looks up the pristine configuration of the current activeViewId within the savedViews array.
2.	It forces committedFilters, columnVisibility, and columnOrder back to those exact saved values.
3.	It syncs the <FilterBar />'s transientFilters back to match.
4.	The isDirty flag drops back to false, the action buttons vanish, and the table automatically re-fetches the original data parameters.
6 & 7. Table column order or visibility is changed
•	The Flow: The user drags a column header to reorder it or toggles a checkbox in the material-react-table column menu.
•	State Handling:
1.	Material-react-table fires its internal state change handlers, which we intercept using callbacks (handleColumnVisibilityChange and handleColumnOrderChange).
2.	These callbacks update columnVisibility and columnOrder directly in our page context.
3.	Because these properties live in the context, the derived isDirty engine compares them against the database-saved view layout. They no longer match, so isDirty becomes true, exposing the Save/Revert actions.
4.	Performance note: Because MRT handles its own rendering layout beautifully, updating these layout states will smoothly update the table grid without triggering a backend data refetch.
8. User selects a new filter in the dropdown, then unselects it
•	The Flow: A user opens a dropdown, checks an item, decides against it, and unchecks it before hitting apply.
•	State Handling:
1.	This entire sequence happens purely within the <FilterBar /> component's transientFilters state.
2.	Because the user never clicked Apply, the page-level context remains completely untouched.
3.	The isDirty flag stays false, the table never goes into a loading state, and the data stays exactly as it was.
9. User changes column order/visibility, then manually changes them back
•	The Flow: The user drags Column A to the right, then drags it right back to its starting position (or opens visibility, hides a column, and unhides it).
•	State Handling:
1.	As the user drags or toggles, the context states (columnOrder / columnVisibility) change dynamically.
2.	Every time they change, our reference check runs: JSON.stringify(columnOrder) === JSON.stringify(activeView.order)
3.	The exact moment the user puts the columns back into their original configurations, the deep-equality check passes.
4.	The isDirty flag seamlessly flips back to false, and the Save/Revert buttons cleanly disappear on their own without the user needing to click anything.

To make the ViewSelector highly reusable across different pages—each with entirely unique filters, data attributes, and table column setups—we must follow a core design principle: The component should be a "dumb" visual shell that accepts its layout, configuration, and state as props or via a generic context.
The component itself should not care what the filters are; it only cares that it has an array of views to display, a way to tell the parent which view is active, and a slot to render the page-specific modals.
Here is the architectural pattern to make this component universally shareable across your app.
________________________________________
1. The Generic View Schema
To ensure the ViewSelector can handle any page, we establish a standardized TypeScript contract (or JSON structure) for what a "View" is. By using a generic type parameter (TFilters), the component remains agnostic to the specific structure of a page's filters.
TypeScript
// types/view.ts
export interface SavedView<TFilters = Record<string, any>> {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  isSystemDefault?: boolean;
  
  // Dynamic page-specific configurations
  filters: TFilters;
  columnVisibility: Record<string, boolean>;
  columnOrder: string[];
}
________________________________________
2. Decoupling Configuration via Composition (The "Slot" Pattern)
Because the "Create View" and "Manage View" popups will look vastly different depending on the page (e.g., the Create View modal on a Users page needs to let them name user-specific filters, while an Invoices page needs date-range filters), we avoid hardcoding modals inside the dropdown.
Instead, we use React compound components or render props to pass the specific management modals down.
The Reusable <ViewSelector /> Component
TypeScript
// components/shared/ViewSelector.tsx
import { ReactNode } from 'react';
import { SavedView } from '@/types/view';

interface ViewSelectorProps<T> {
  views: SavedView<T>[];
  activeViewId: string | null;
  isDirty: boolean;
  onViewChange: (viewId: string) => void;
  onSave: () => void;
  onRevert: () => void;
  
  // Slots for Page-Specific Modals/Popups
  createViewElement: ReactNode; 
  manageViewsElement: ReactNode;
}

export function ViewSelector<T>({
  views,
  activeViewId,
  isDirty,
  onViewChange,
  onSave,
  onRevert,
  createViewElement,
  manageViewsElement,
}: ViewSelectorProps<T>) {
  const activeView = views.find((v) => v.id === activeViewId);
  const dropdownLabel = activeView 
    ? `${activeView.name}${isDirty ? ' *' : ''}` 
    : 'Select View';

  return (
    <div className="flex items-center gap-3">
      {/* Reusable UI Dropdown Shell */}
      <Dropdown menuLabel={dropdownLabel}>
        {/* Dynamic List of Views */}
        <DropdownGroup>
          {views.map((view) => (
            <DropdownItem 
              key={view.id} 
              isActive={view.id === activeViewId}
              onClick={() => onViewChange(view.id)}
            >
              {view.name}
            </DropdownItem>
          ))}
        </DropdownGroup>
        
        <DropdownDivider />
        
        {/* Dropdown Footer - Slots are rendered here */}
        <DropdownFooter>
          {createViewElement}
          {manageViewsElement}
        </DropdownFooter>
      </Dropdown>

      {/* Conditional Action Buttons */}
      {isDirty && (
        <div className="flex gap-2 animation-fade-in">
          <Button onClick={onSave} variant="primary" size="sm">Save Changes</Button>
          <Button onClick={onRevert} variant="secondary" size="sm">Revert</Button>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
