4. Key Takeaways for Multi-Page Scaling
1.	State Isolation: The ViewSelector stays 100% presentation-driven. It doesn't know how to save a view or fetch views from an API. It just triggers callbacks (onSave, onViewChange) provided by whichever page hosts it.
2.	Type Safety via Generics: By utilizing <ViewSelector<TFilters>>, your code editors will properly auto-complete and protect the shape of your configurations across different feature directories.
3.	Flexible Modals: By leveraging React's children/slots pattern (createViewElement), the dropdown footer links simply trigger modals that live alongside their respective feature domains, avoiding any bloated conditional logic inside your shared UI components directory.
That is an excellent observation. If the business requirements for creating and managing views are uniform across the app—meaning every page simply saves a name, an icon/color, or a description, while the dashboard context quietly injects the payload details behind the scenes—then we can turn <CreateViewModal /> and <ManageViewsModal /> into fully global, highly reusable components too.
To pull this off, the global modals just need to be fed two things:
1.	The current configuration payload (supplied by the active page's context).
2.	An API configuration or callback handler to perform the save/delete network requests.
Here is how you can centralize the entire system so that adding a "Saved Views" feature to a brand-new page takes less than 10 lines of code.
________________________________________
1. Unified API/State Strategy via Callbacks
Since the global modals shouldn't be coupled to a specific page's API endpoints, we pass standard save and delete operation callbacks. This allows the modal to remain a presentation shell while the page's local layout controls the data execution.
The Reusable <CreateViewModal />
This modal only cares about capturing user inputs like the View Name. When the user clicks "Save", it hands that name back to the parent along with the payload.
TypeScript
// components/shared/views/CreateViewModal.tsx
import { useState } from 'react';

interface CreateViewModalProps {
  isOpen: boolean;
  onClose: () => void;
  // Callback that passes the custom name back up to the page feature
  onConfirmCreate: (viewName: string) => Promise<void>;
}

export function CreateViewModal({ isOpen, onClose, onConfirmCreate }: CreateViewModalProps) {
  const [viewName, setViewName] = useState('');
  const [isSubmitting, setIsSubmitting] = useState(false);

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  const handleSubmit = async (e: React.FormEvent) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    if (!viewName.trim()) return;

    setIsSubmitting(true);
    try {
      await onConfirmCreate(viewName);
      setViewName('');
      onClose();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Failed to create view:", error);
    } finally {
      setIsSubmitting(false);
    }
  };

  return (
    <Modal isOpen={isOpen} onClose={onClose} title="Create New View">
      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit} className="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <Input 
          label="View Name" 
          value={viewName} 
          onChange={(e) => setViewName(e.target.value)} 
          placeholder="e.g., Q3 High Priority Orders"
          required
        />
        <div className="flex justify-end gap-2">
          <Button type="button" variant="secondary" onClick={onClose}>Cancel</Button>
          <Button type="submit" variant="primary" isLoading={isSubmitting}>Create View</Button>
        </div>
      </form>
    </Modal>
  );
}
