Why this extension shines:
•	True Zero-Bleed Isolation: The shared view components handle all operational layouts, and the page context handles all data transformation logic.
•	Maintainability: If the UI/UX team decides to change the look of the "Manage View" popup layout down the road, you only have to modify it in one file, and every dashboard across your whole application instantly updates.

To make this work seamlessly across different pages while keeping your types clean and secure, we design the Context API using TypeScript Generics.
By defining a generic context factory or a reusable structural interface, each page can initialize its own standalone Context Provider, passing its specific FilterType down into the engine.
Here is exactly how to design, define, and instantiate that Context API.
________________________________________
1. The Generic Context State Definition
First, we define a structured TypeScript interface for the exact shape of our page context. We use a generic parameter F to represent the unique shape of that page's filters.
TypeScript
// types/dashboardContext.ts
import { SavedView } from '@/types/view';

export interface DashboardContextState<F> {
  // 1. Views Array & Tracking
  views: SavedView<F>[];
  activeViewId: string | null;
  isDirty: boolean;

  // 2. Committed Configurations (What the Table & API read)
  committedFilters: F;
  columnVisibility: Record<string, boolean>;
  columnOrder: string[];

  // 3. Transient Selections (What the user changes BEFORE hitting Apply)
  transientFilters: F;
  setTransientFilters: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<F>>;

  // 4. Global UI States
  isLoading: boolean;

  // 5. Shared Orcheration Callbacks (Fed directly into ViewSelector & Table)
  changeView: (viewId: string) => void;
  applyTransientFilters: () => void;
  resetTransientFilters: () => void;
  saveCurrentView: () => Promise<void>;
  revertToSaved: () => void;
  createNewView: (name: string) => Promise<void>;
  deleteView: (id: string) => Promise<void>;
  
  // 6. Material-React-Table Bound Handlers
  setColumnVisibility: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<Record<string, boolean>>>;
  setColumnOrder: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<string[]>>;
}
