Core Structural Features of This Code:
1.	Unconstrained Generic Flag (<T>): The declaration uses standard React typescript generic type arguments. Notice that the payload schema signature maps dynamically as views: SavedView<T>[]. The component itself has zero knowledge about your database data layers, making it highly reusable.
2.	Encapsulated Popups: The modal open flags (isCreateOpen, isManageOpen) live entirely within the internal state hooks here. The hosting layout page doesn't have to keep track of any modal variables or click states.
3.	Optimistic Loading Protection: The Save Changes handler sets local state to isSaving instantly upon trigger. This prevents double-clicks from calling your database multiple times if network responses are slow.
4.	Visual Context Feedback: The dropdown button dynamically updates its color profile to a warm amber background when isDirty is true, providing clear feedback that there are unsaved layout or filter changes.
Here is the complete, modular TypeScript definition for the DashboardContextState.
This interface defines the operational contract for your localized state engine. It uses a TypeScript generic (F) to represent the page-specific filter structure, allowing the exact same context skeleton to safely support any dashboard across your app.
The Context Type Definitions (types/dashboardContext.ts)
TypeScript
import React from 'react';
import { SavedView } from './view';

/**
 * Generic blueprint managing state distribution across rows 1, 2, and 3.
 * @template F The custom object shape representing a unique page's filter fields.
 */
export interface DashboardContextState<F> {
  // ==========================================
  // 1. VIEW CONFIGURATION ENGINE (ROW 1)
  // ==========================================
  /** Collection of views available for this dashboard fetched from the API */
  views: SavedView<F>[];
  
  /** The currently selected view ID. Null means factory defaults are applied */
  activeViewId: string | null;
  
  /** Evaluates to true if current layout parameters don't match the database view configuration */
  isDirty: boolean;

  // ==========================================
  // 2. COMMITTED DATA & LAYOUTS (ROW 3 / API)
  // ==========================================
  /** Filter values currently driving data fetches and API parameterization */
  committedFilters: F;
  
  /** Material-React-Table controlled layout visibility schema */
  columnVisibility: Record<string, boolean>;
  
  /** Material-React-Table controlled layout order tracking list */
  columnOrder: string[];
  
  /** Global loading overlay flag tied to data-layer requests */
  isLoading: boolean;

  // ==========================================
  // 3. TRANSIENT DROPDOWN CONTROLS (ROW 2)
  // ==========================================
  /** Temporary, uncommitted filter changes sitting inside dropdown states before 'Apply' is pressed */
  transientFilters: F;
  
  /** Local state dispatcher for mutating temporary dropdown filter checkboxes/inputs */
  setTransientFilters: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<F>>;

  // ==========================================
  // 4. ACTION CALLBACK HANDLERS
  // ==========================================
  /** Switches view profile and flushes its saved parameters down to filters and table states */
  changeView: (viewId: string) => void;
  
  /** Commits temporary dropdown selections into live query filters, triggering an API fetch */
  applyTransientFilters: () => void;
  
  /** flushes filter profiles back to their factory layout settings */
  resetTransientFilters: () => void;
  
  /** Makes an API PUT/POST request updating the database configuration with current view parameters */
  saveCurrentView: () => Promise<void>;
  
  /** Discards all temporary table mutations, pulling pristine layouts back from the active view object */
  revertToSaved: () => void;
  
  /** Accepts a target label name, serializes current layout states, and posts a new view object to the server */
  createNewView: (name: string) => Promise<void>;
  
  /** Removes an entry from the database layer and falls back cleanly to primary defaults */
  deleteView: (id: string) => Promise<void>;

  // ==========================================
  // 5. MATERIAL-REACT-TABLE STATE HOOKS
  // ==========================================
  /** Directly intercept and bind material-react-table's column visibility setters */
  setColumnVisibility: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<Record<string, boolean>>>;
  
  /** Directly intercept and bind material-react-table's column layout reordering setters */
  setColumnOrder: React.Dispatch<React.SetStateAction<string[]>>;
}
