Architectural Benefits of This Contract
•	Clean Context Segregation: By dividing state into transientFilters (Row 2 UI updates) and committedFilters (Row 3 API executions), you prevent input lag.
•	Typing Flexibility: When declaring a context file for a dashboard, your code looks exceptionally clean:
TypeScript
// Example inside an Payments Dashboard Context definition
const PaymentsDashboardContext = createContext<DashboardContextState<PaymentFilters> | undefined>(undefined);
•	Seamless MRT Interception: The inclusion of setColumnVisibility and setColumnOrder makes hooking into material-react-table state change handlers native and frictionless.
This is a common requirement in enterprise software: a single page might host multiple variations of a workspace (e.g., a "Tickets" page that toggles between "Bug Items" and "Feature Request Items," each requiring completely different filters and columns). Meanwhile, other pages remain simple and don't need any sub-type variant.
To cleanly handle this without breaking our architectural model, we can treat the subType as an optional configuration tier in our database schema and context.
Here is how we adapt the types, the Context API, and the dashboard implementation to handle pages with or without sub-types seamlessly.
________________________________________
1. Updated Generic View Schema
We update the SavedView structure to optionally include screenName and subType. If a page doesn't have a sub-type, subType simply defaults to undefined or a standard string fallback like "DEFAULT".
TypeScript
