Architectural Wrap-up
By placing the responsibility of subType evaluation directly within the local page context providers, your base layouts remain clean and highly performant. Complex multi-flavored pages isolate their sub-type sorting logic internally, while simple screens remain unburdened by properties they don't need.
To answer your question directly: No, the <ViewSelector /> should absolutely not make the API call to fetch the list of views. If the view selector made its own API calls, it would become tightly coupled to a specific database endpoint. It would no longer be a reusable shared component, and you wouldn't be able to drop it onto different pages with different workflows.
The actual API network transactions should happen at the Page Context level (via custom hooks called inside your Context Provider). The Context behaves as the brain, while components like the view selector, filter bar, and table act as the hands and eyes.
Here is the exact data-fetching architecture for the 3 distinct data streams: Views, Filter Dropdown values, and Table Data.
________________________________________
The Data Fetching Architecture
To keep the page highly performant and ensure the table doesn't reload stutteringly, we segregate data fetching into two categories: Static/Lookups (load once on page mount) and Dynamic (re-fetch when parameters change).
                 [ Page Router / Parent Entrypoint ]
                                 │
                   ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
                   ▼                           ▼
       [ Page Context Provider ] ───► [ Initial Static Hook ]
                   │                     (Fetch Views & Dropdown Options)
                   │
         ┌─────────┼─────────┐
         ▼         ▼         ▼
     Row 1 UI  Row 2 UI  [ Dynamic Table Hook ] ◄── (Re-fetches on 'Apply')
   (Selector)  (Filters)     └─► Row 3 UI (Table)
1. Where to Fetch the List of Views
•	Location: Inside a custom hook (e.g., useFetchSavedViews) executed inside the Page Context Provider.
•	The Logic: On page mount, this hook makes an API call passing the current screenName (and optionally subType). It stores the result in the context's allViews state. The context then distributes this array to the <ViewSelector /> via simple props.
2. Where to Fetch the Filter Dropdown Options
•	Location: Inside the Page Context Provider or the wrapper Filter Bar Component.
•	The Logic: Options for your dropdowns (e.g., the list of all possible "Statuses", "Regions", or "Assignees") are typically lookup data. They should be fetched once when the page loads.
•	Pro Tip: If multiple dropdowns depend on each other (e.g., selecting Region A changes the options in the City dropdown), fetch this data at the Context level so the cascading logic can be calculated cleanly before hitting the UI.
3. Where to Fetch the Table Data
•	Location: Inside a dynamic custom hook (e.g., useFetchTableData) executed directly inside the Table Container Component or the Context Provider, reactive only to committedFilters.
•	The Logic: This hook monitors committedFilters. When the page first loads, it uses the default view's filters. When a user clicks Apply, committedFilters updates, instantly causing this hook to trigger a new Axios/Fetch request to get the fresh dataset.
________________________________________
Architectural Implementation Example
Here is how this setup looks inside your feature folder, cleanly separating your API network requests from your UI presentation:
TypeScript
// features/orders/context/OrdersDashboardContext.tsx
import React, { createContext, useContext, useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { useFetchOrdersLookups } from '../hooks/useFetchOrdersLookups';
import { useFetchPageViews } from '../hooks/useFetchPageViews';

export const OrdersDashboardProvider: React.FC<{ children: React.ReactNode }> = ({ children }) => {
  const [currentSubType, setCurrentSubType] = useState('B2B_ORDERS');
  
  // 1. Fetch Lookups (Dropdown Values) once on mount
  const { statuses, regions, lookupsLoading } = useFetchOrdersLookups(currentSubType);

  // 2. Fetch Saved Views once on mount (or when subType changes)
  const { allViews, viewsLoading, setAllViews } = useFetchPageViews('ORDERS_PAGE');

  // Local Layout states
  const [activeViewId, setActiveViewId] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [committedFilters, setCommittedFilters] = useState({});
  const [transientFilters, setTransientFilters] = useState({});
  const [columnVisibility, setColumnVisibility] = useState({});
  const [columnOrder, setColumnOrder] = useState<string[]>([]);

  // ... sync functions (changeView, applyTransientFilters, etc.)

  const isLoading = lookupsLoading || viewsLoading;

  return (
    <OrdersDashboardContext.Provider value={{
      views: allViews.filter(v => v.subType === currentSubType),
      dropdownOptions: { statuses, regions }, // Passed down to Row 2 Filter Dropdowns
      committedFilters,
      isLoading,
      // ... rest of shared state
    }}>
      {children}
    </OrdersDashboardContext.Provider>
  );
};
And then, inside Row 3 (The Table Container), you isolate the actual data row queries:
TypeScript
// features/orders/components/Row3DataTable.tsx
import { useOrdersDashboard } from '../context/OrdersDashboardContext';
import { useFetchOrdersGridRows } from '../hooks/useFetchOrdersGridRows';

export function Row3DataTable() {
  const { committedFilters, currentSubType, columnVisibility, columnOrder } = useOrdersDashboard();

  // 3. This hook runs ONLY when committedFilters or subType changes. 
  // Dragging columns or toggling local checkboxes will NOT trigger this network request.
  const { rows, rowsLoading } = useFetchOrdersGridRows(currentSubType, committedFilters);

  const table = useMaterialReactTable({
    columns: myColumns,
    data: rows ?? [],
    state: { 
      isLoading: rowsLoading,
      columnVisibility,
      columnOrder 
    },
    // ...
  });

  return <MaterialReactTable table={table} />;
}
