Why this Architecture Wins on Performance & Maintenance
1.	Separation of Concerns: <ViewSelector /> is 100% presentation-driven. You can take this component and place it on a "Users" page, an "Inventory" page, or a "Settings" page without rewriting a single line of internal code.
2.	Network Request Minimization: Because the table fetching hook sits below or listens exclusively to committedFilters, users can click around inside the filter dropdown options endlessly without triggering premature backend database requests.
3.	Streamlined Skeletal States: Because your lookup metadata states (isLoading) are held at the top level, you can cleanly show a single unified loading skeleton on initial page load, transitioning cleanly to micro-spinners inside the table once the layout has initialized.
To bring this senior-architected system to life, we will build out the custom hooks layer. By cleanly abstracting our asynchronous workflows into dedicated custom hooks, we keep our React Context components completely unburdened by fetch-and-catch blocks.
Here is the production-ready code for the three custom data hooks running our architecture:
1.	useFetchPageViews (Fetches saved view snapshots)
2.	useFetchOrdersLookups (Fetches dropdown selector options)
3.	useFetchOrdersGridRows (Fetches the actual table row items)
________________________________________
1. useFetchPageViews.ts (Row 1 Views Engine)
This hook fetches all saved configurations matching the parent dashboard asset key. It returns utilities to optimism-update local arrays immediately upon executing CRUD mutations.
TypeScript
// features/orders/hooks/useFetchPageViews.ts
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { SavedView } from '@/types/view';

export function useFetchPageViews(screenName: string) {
  const [allViews, setAllViews] = useState<SavedView<any>[]>([]);
  const [viewsLoading, setViewsLoading] = useState<boolean>(true);
  const [viewsError, setViewsError] = useState<Error | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let isMounted = true;
    
    async function loadViews() {
      setViewsLoading(true);
      try {
        // In production, substitute with your network client instance:
        // const response = await api.get(`/api/v1/views?screenName=${screenName}`);
        // const data = response.data;
        
        // Mocking API Network database latency simulation:
        await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 600));
        
        const mockData: SavedView<any>[] = [
          {
            id: 'v1',
            name: 'Default Pipeline View',
            screenName: 'ORDERS_PAGE',
            subType: 'B2B_ORDERS',
            isSystemDefault: true,
            filters: { status: ['pending'], minAmount: 100 },
            columnVisibility: { id: true, trackingNumber: true, amount: true },
            columnOrder: ['id', 'trackingNumber', 'amount']
          },
          {
            id: 'v2',
            name: 'High Value Handlers',
            screenName: 'ORDERS_PAGE',
            subType: 'B2B_ORDERS',
            filters: { status: ['pending', 'shipped'], minAmount: 5000 },
            columnVisibility: { id: true, amount: true },
            columnOrder: ['id', 'amount']
          }
        ];

        if (isMounted) {
          setAllViews(mockData);
          setViewsError(null);
        }
      } catch (err) {
        if (isMounted) setViewsError(err as Error);
      } finally {
        if (isMounted) setViewsLoading(false);
      }
    }

    loadViews();

    return () => {
      isMounted = false;
    };
  }, [screenName]);

  return { allViews, setAllViews, viewsLoading, viewsError };
}
