Architectural Review: Why This Structure Works
•	Aborts and Cleanup Racing: Both useFetchPageViews and useFetchOrdersGridRows leverage localized block scopes tracking standard isMounted state toggles. If a user spam-switches views or clicks away from the dashboard entirely, unreturned thread processes will safely close out without triggering memory leaks or unexpected React state adjustments on unmounted instances.
•	Granular Network Control: By wrapping these into distinct hooks rather than a single monolithic useEffect in the main page component, you guarantee that loading lookups once does not forcefully invoke loading table data rows again, maintaining optimal rendering speeds across the application.
________________________________________

To successfully build this highly performant dashboard with zero reliance on heavy state libraries, we applied a combination of structural, transactional, and optimization-focused React concepts.
Here is a breakdown of the key architectural concepts used to pull this off:
________________________________________
1. Separation of Container and Presentational Components ("Dumb" vs. "Smart")
The <ViewSelector /> component is written as a Presentational Component. It knows nothing about OrderFilters, API URLs, or column shapes. It receives arrays, statuses, and generic callback handlers (onSaveCurrentView, onViewChange) entirely through props. This abstraction is what allows the exact same component to be used across different pages with completely different filter controls.
2. Transactional State Architecture (Committed vs. Transient)
If we bound our filter dropdown checkboxes directly to the core dashboard state, every single mouse click would force the entire page, the API hooks, and material-react-table to lag and re-render.
To solve this, we split our data flow into a transactional model:
•	Transient State (transientFilters): Localized strictly to the filter controls row. It acts as a draft area while the user checks or unchecks options.
•	Committed State (committedFilters): The source of truth that the table grid and the data-fetching hooks watch. The draft state is flushed into the committed state only when the user explicitly clicks the Apply button.
3. Derived State (Eliminating useEffect Synchronizations)
A junior approach to managing the isDirty flag might involve writing a complex useEffect that constantly runs deep comparisons and manages a setIsDirty(true) state hook.
Instead, we used Derived State via useMemo. Because the context has access to both the raw active view from the database and the user's live table adjustments, the isDirty variable is calculated instantly on-the-fly during render:
TypeScript
const isDirty = JSON.stringify(committedFilters) !== JSON.stringify(activeView.filters);
If the user manually reverts their changes or checks the box back to its original state, isDirty instantly flips back to false, causing the Save/Revert buttons to disappear without triggering an extra state mutation cycle.
4. Context API as a Localized Feature State Machine
Instead of polluting global application layers (like Redux or a root-level provider) with temporary page UI details, we leverage a Localized Context. The <OrdersDashboardProvider> wraps only this specific page dashboard template. The moment the user navigates away to a completely different part of the application, this entire feature context is instantly destroyed and unmounted, cleanly wiping out all memory allocations.
5. Referential Stability Control (useMemo and useCallback)
Grid systems like material-react-table are highly sensitive to object reference changes. If you pass an inline array or a raw object down to the table on every render, it interprets it as new data, creating infinite re-render loops.
•	useMemo was used inside the table component to freeze the structural definitions of the columns.
•	useCallback (or stable context references) ensure that the column ordering and visibility functions passed to the table remain identical between renders unless explicitly changed by user interaction.
6. Declarative Data Fetching Hooks
By encapsulating your network logic into useFetchOrdersGridRows, we made data-fetching declarative rather than imperative. The hook declares its dependencies ([subType, committedFilters]). Instead of writing manual fetch triggers inside component buttons, the hook naturally reacts to adjustments made to the committed state context, keeping the application's side effects predictable and safe from race conditions.
Here are the structural insights on how our component count balances performance, maintainability, and reusability.
________________________________________
1. Breakdown of Created Components
In our final working code, we created 5 core components (excluding the global modals):
1.	OrdersDashboardPage (The Parent Orchestrator)
2.	Row1ViewManagement (View Context Shell)
3.	ViewSelector (The Generic, Shared Dropdown UI)
4.	Row2FilterBar (The Transient Filter Controls)
5.	Row3DataTable (The Presentation Table Container)
________________________________________
2. One Monolithic Filter Component vs. Separated Components
Combining Row 1 (Views) and Row 2 (Filter Options) into a single, massive component is a tempting shortcut, but separating them was absolutely the right move. Here is why keeping them decoupled is a superior architectural choice:
The Problem with a Monolithic Component
If the ViewSelector and the FilterBar were combined, you would lose the ability to share the view architecture across the app. The view selector would become bloated with layout instructions for checkboxes, date-pickers, and numeric fields unique to that specific page.
The Benefit of Separation
By keeping them isolated, ViewSelector stays 100% agnostic. It can be moved into a /components/shared folder and dropped onto an "Invoices" page, a "Users" page, or a "Products" page.
Furthermore, re-render boundaries are maintained. When a user types a number into the filter fields in Row 2, only the Row2FilterBar re-renders. The ViewSelector in Row 1 remains completely untouched, saving CPU cycles.
________________________________________
3. Recommendations for Separating Logic Between Components
When deciding where to place logic, use the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) and the concept of State Co-location.
The Rule of Thumb for State placement
•	Transient UI State belongs in the Leaf Component: If a piece of state is only used to control a dropdown opening/closing, a modal displaying, or an uncommitted checkbox toggle, keep it using useState inside that exact component.
•	Committed State belongs in the Page Context: If a piece of state needs to be read by the API to fetch rows, or determines if a global "Save" button is active, lift it up into the local Page Context.
Decoupling Event Handlers
Components should never execute data logic directly. Instead, they should emit semantic actions.
•	Bad: A dropdown button component calling an Axios instance directly.
•	Good: A dropdown emitting an onViewChange(id) event callback prop, leaving the implementation details to the context provider.
________________________________________
4. When is "Less Components" Better vs. "More Components"?
The choice between creating fewer components or dividing them into smaller ones depends on Reusability and Cognitive Load.
When "Less Components" (A Single Unified File) is Better
•	Premature Optimization: Do not break a small layout into 10 files if those sub-components are only 5 lines of code and will never be reused anywhere else. It forces developers to constantly jump between files just to understand a simple layout.
•	Tight Domain Coupling: If Component B cannot exist or function without Component A, keeping them in the same file as separate functions (as we did in our sandbox) is perfectly fine. It keeps the feature localized and readable.
When "More Components" (Deep Splitting) is Better
•	To Prevent Unnecessary Re-renders: If a highly active piece of state (like a real-time text input search or a countdown timer) lives inside a parent, it will force everything below it to re-render. Splitting that active input out into its own component insulates the rest of the page from those state changes.
•	To Promote Global Reusability: If a piece of UI (like a Button, a Modal framework, or our generic ViewSelector) performs a purely functional task that other screens can benefit from, extract it immediately.
•	To Reduce High Complexity: If a single component's file surpasses 300–400 lines of code, it becomes difficult to maintain. Break it down logically by rows or sections so that the code remains scannable.


Moving our useState and useEffect blocks out of the components and tucking them neatly into custom hooks (like useFetchPageViews or useFetchOrdersGridRows) is a deliberate architectural pattern called Separation of Concerns.
If we had written all those useEffect and data-fetching fetch blocks directly inside our components, the file would quickly become a tangled, unreadable mess.
Here is the exact engineering breakdown of why custom hooks are vastly superior to dropping raw useState and useEffect blocks into your layout code.
________________________________________
1. Drastically Lowering Component Cognitive Load
A React component has one primary job: to declare what the UI should look like based on data. When you mix UI code with data-orchestration side effects, developers have to parse through lines of state setups and asynchronous try/catch blocks just to figure out where a <div> ends.
By extracting the logic, our components remain highly readable presentation maps:
TypeScript
// This is pure, readable intent. No tracking variables or error parsing blocks in sight.
function Row3DataTable() {
  const { currentSubType, committedFilters } = useDashboard();
  const { rows, rowsLoading } = useFetchOrdersGridRows(currentSubType, committedFilters);

  return <MaterialReactTable data={rows} state={{ isLoading: rowsLoading }} ... />;
}
________________________________________
2. Eliminating the Dreaded useEffect Race Conditions
Managing raw asynchronous networks inside standard component useEffect wrappers is notorious for introducing bugs called race conditions.
Imagine a user rapidly clicking between View A and View B. If the network request for View A takes 2 seconds, but View B takes 0.5 seconds, the user will see View B's data briefly pop up, only to be overwritten a moment later when View A's slow request finally resolves.
When we bundle this into a custom hook, we can elegantly isolate clean-up subscriptions out of sight:
TypeScript
// Inside our custom hook—clean-up logic is safely managed away from the layout
useEffect(() => {
  let isMounted = true;

  async function fetchDataset() {
    const data = await apiCall();
    if (isMounted) setRows(data); // If the user clicked away, this is safely ignored!
  }

  fetchDataset();
  return () => { isMounted = false; }; // Cancels the state update if dependencies change
}, [committedFilters]);
________________________________________
3. Creating Rigid "Re-render Guards"
React components re-render whenever their internal states or parent states tick. If you place a useEffect inside a massive layout component, any accidental state change in a neighbor element could prematurely trigger your side effects.
By moving data fetching into its own custom hook with an explicit dependency array ([subType, committedFilters]), we create a strict execution boundary. We guarantee to React that nothing happening on the rest of the page—whether a user is reordering a table column, opening a popup modal, or typing in a field—can force a backend API hit until those specific committed filters change.
________________________________________
4. Testability and Portability
Testing raw useEffect blocks inside a UI component requires rendering the entire virtual DOM tree, mocking the table wrappers, and simulating user events just to see if an API call gets fired correctly.
When logic lives inside a standalone custom hook, you can test your page's data lifecycle independently of the UI layout using standard utilities like @testing-library/react-hooks:
TypeScript
// Your unit test can verify data states without ever rendering a single HTML element
const { result, waitForNextUpdate } = renderHook(() => useFetchPageViews('ORDERS_PAGE'));
await waitForNextUpdate();
expect(result.current.allViews).toHaveLength(2);
________________________________________
Summary Checklist: When to write a Custom Hook
•	Use useState / useEffect inside the component: When the state is purely graphical and superficial (e.g., is a dropdown menu open? Is a text field currently focused?).
•	Extract into a Custom Hook: When the state coordinates synchronous data mutations, talks to a network API database, or orchestrates side effects that rely on business data.


To truly understand why useEffect race conditions are so dreaded, we have to look at the asynchronous nature of the web.
When you trigger a data fetch inside a standard React component, you are launching an asynchronous operation into the background. React has absolutely no built-in awareness of network speeds. It doesn't know—or care—which network request was fired first; it only cares about which network response arrives last.
Here is a deep dive into how race conditions happen, why they break applications, and how custom hooks elegantly eliminate them.
________________________________________
1. The Anatomy of a Race Condition Bug
Imagine a user is clicking through a sidebar to view different project dashboards.
1.	The user clicks on Project A. A network request (Request #1) is fired to fetch Project A's data.
2.	The network bottlenecks or the database takes a moment to process Request #1.
3.	Impatiently, the user clicks on Project B. React changes the state, re-renders, and fires a brand-new network request (Request #2) to fetch Project B's data.
4.	Request #2 resolves incredibly fast (perhaps it was cached or the dataset is tiny). The component updates, and the UI correctly displays Project B's data.
5.	Suddenly, 500ms later, the slow Request #1 for Project A finally finishes. Because the component's internal state setter (setData) is still blindly waiting for that promise to resolve, it fires.
The Result: The UI text header says "Project B", but the data table underneath suddenly flickers and displays Project A's data. The application is now in a broken, corrupted state.
________________________________________
2. Visualizing the Race Condition Timeline
As shown in the timeline below, the linear order of responses overwrites the chronological order of intent:
User Click:      [Click Project A] ────────────────────► [Click Project B]
                        │                                      │
Network Flight:         ▼ (Request #1: Slow)                   ▼ (Request #2: Fast)
                        ███████████████████████████████████▓   █████████▓
                                                           │            │
API Resolves:                                              │            ▼
                                                           │     [UI Shows Project B]
                                                           ▼     (Correct)
                                                    [UI Shows Project A] 
                                                    (CORRUPTED OVERWRITE!)
________________________________________
3. How Custom Hooks Kill Race Conditions
To eliminate this bug, we must ensure that when a component's dependencies change (or when the component unmounts), any older, uncompleted network requests are stripped of their permission to update the React state.
We accomplish this using two main patterns inside our custom hooks: The Boolean Flag Pattern or The AbortController API.
Pattern A: The Boolean Flag Pattern (Clean & Fail-Safe)
This is the lightweight approach we used in our sandbox hooks. It leverages the cleanup function of React's useEffect.
TypeScript
export function useFetchOrdersGridRows(subType: string, committedFilters: OrderFilters) {
  const [rows, setRows] = useState<OrderRowItem[]>([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    // 1. Initialize a local block-scoped flag set to true
    let isCurrent = true;

    async function fetchDataset() {
      const data = await api.get('/orders', { subType, ...committedFilters });
      
      // 3. Only update state if this specific effect cycle is still the active one!
      if (isCurrent) {
        setRows(data);
      }
    }

    fetchDataset();

    // 2. THE CLEANUP: React runs this the EXACT millisecond the dependencies change
    return () => {
      isCurrent = false; // Permanently cancels this specific fetch's permission to update state
    };
  }, [subType, committedFilters]);

  return { rows };
}
How it works: When the user switches from Project A to Project B, React immediately triggers the cleanup function of Project A's effect, instantly flipping its local isCurrent flag to false. When Project A's slow network request eventually returns, the condition if (isCurrent) fails, and the stale data is safely dropped into the void.
Pattern B: The AbortController API (The Gold Standard)
While the boolean flag prevents stale state updates, the browser is still technically forced to download the entire useless payload in the background. If you want to be highly optimized, you can use the browser's native AbortController to physically cancel the network request over the wire.
TypeScript
export function useFetchOrdersGridRows(subType: string, committedFilters: OrderFilters) {
  const [rows, setRows] = useState<OrderRowItem[]>([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    // 1. Create an instance of the browser's controller
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const { signal } = controller;

    async function fetchDataset() {
      try {
        // 2. Pass the abort signal down to your fetch/axios client
        const response = await fetch(`/api/orders?subType=${subType}`, { signal });
        const data = await response.json();
        
        setRows(data);
      } catch (error: any) {
        if (error.name === 'AbortError') {
          console.log('Fetch successfully aborted by the hook guard.');
        } else {
          // Handle legitimate errors
        }
      }
    }

    fetchDataset();

    // 3. THE CLEANUP: Abort the actual network request instantly if dependencies shift
    return () => {
      controller.abort();
    };
  }, [subType, committedFilters]);

  return { rows };
}
How it works: The millisecond the user changes a filter or swaps tabs, the browser immediately severs the HTTP connection for the pending request. You save user bandwidth, reduce server strain, and guarantee that the UI stays perfectly synchronized with the user's latest intent.

Yes, absolutely! Custom hooks follow a very specific lifecycle because they are bound directly to the lifecycle of the React component that calls them.
A common misconception is that custom hooks have a separate lifecycle from the component. In reality, a custom hook is not an isolated container; it is a macro that injects state and effects directly into the host component. If a component re-renders, the custom hook code executes again from top to bottom.
To understand exactly when your code runs, let's trace the execution timeline of our useFetchOrdersGridRows hook through a complete lifecycle: Mounting, Updating (The Re-render Guard), and Unmounting.
________________________________________
The Complete Hook Lifecycle Timeline
Here is step-by-step what happens under the hood when your page runs:
1. The Mount Phase (Initial Load)
When the user navigates to your dashboard, the host component mounts.
•	Line-by-Line Execution: React executes the hook function from the top. It initializes the useState slots (rows as an empty array, rowsLoading as false).
•	The Return: The hook immediately returns those initial values so the component can draw its initial setup (e.g., showing a table layout with a loading spinner).
•	The Side Effect: After the browser paints that initial setup, React looks at the useEffect block. Because it is the initial mount, the effect fires. It sets rowsLoading to true and triggers the asynchronous API network fetch in the background.
•	The Resolution: Once the API network request resolves 600ms later, the hook calls setRows(mockData). This state change forces the host component to re-render with the fresh data rows.
________________________________________
2. The Update Phase (The Re-render Guard)
This is where the magic of the dependency array [subType, committedFilters] comes into play.
Suppose a user is actively interacting with the dashboard. They check a box in the filter dropdown (Row 2) or drag a table column to resize it (Row 3).
•	The Re-render Trigger: These UI actions cause states to shift, forcing the component—and the custom hook code—to re-execute from top to bottom.
•	The Skip Condition: When the hook executes this time, React evaluates the values in the useEffect dependency array. If subType and committedFilters are exactly the same as they were on the last render, React completely skips the useEffect block.
•	The Performance Win: The internal API network fetch is not called again. The hook simply returns the already-cached rows array instantly. Typing, dragging, or checking un-applied boxes runs at a smooth 60% frames-per-second, perfectly insulated from network lags.
________________________________________
3. The Transaction Phase (Applying Filters)
Now, the user hits the Apply Filters button.
•	The Commit: The context transforms transientFilters into committedFilters.
•	The Effect Cleanup: Because a value in the dependency array changed, React prepares to rerun the effect. But first, it executes the cleanup function from the previous fetch execution cycle (return () => { isMounted = false }). This cancels any old, lingering requests.
•	The Fetch Rerun: React fires the useEffect block again with the brand-new filter parameters, pulling the fresh target rows from your backend.
________________________________________
4. The Unmount Phase (Leaving the Page)
When the user clicks a link to visit a completely different page (e.g., the Settings page), the dashboard component unmounts.
•	The Final Cleanup: The custom hook is destroyed along with the component. React runs the useEffect cleanup function one final time.
•	The Safe Drop: If a network request was still flying through cyberspace when the user left, isMounted flips to false. When the request eventually arrives, it safely hits the floor without trying to update a non-existent component, preventing memory leaks and application crashes.
________________________________________
Architectural Rule of Thumb
Event	What the Hook Does	What the Component Does
Component Mounts	Initializes local state; fires API effect.	Renders layout skeletons.
User types / drags columns	Executes top-to-bottom but skips the API effect.	Renders instant visual updates.
User clicks "Apply Filters"	Cleans up the old fetch; fires a fresh API fetch.	Displays a loading spinner over rows.
Component Unmounts	Runs final effect cleanup; destroys states.	Disappears from the screen entirely.



To deliver a premium user experience (UX) for a data-dense engineering application, the user interface (UI) needs to move away from the basic, blocky forms of standard templates and adopt an intentional workspace layout.
When dealing with saved views, complex filters, and large data tables, a user’s biggest complaints are typically visual noise, layout shifts, and accidental loss of changes.
Here is an optimized UX design strategy for your layout, designed to maximize data readability, minimize friction, and give users total control over their workspaces.
