1. Visual Layout Wireframe Hierarchy
An enterprise control page should read predictably from top to bottom, keeping global contexts high and operational controls localized.
 
________________________________________
2. Row-by-Row UX Enhancements
Row 1: The Context & Workspace Header
•	The Sub-Type Selector: Do not hide the page sub-types (e.g., B2B vs B2C) inside a deep dropdown. Treat them as a Segmented Control Slidertab or a distinct Button Group on the far right. Switching sub-types completely swaps out the underlying grid rules, so it needs to feel like changing independent channels.
•	The View Selector Dropdown:
o	Give it a distinct visual weight. If it contains unsaved modifications, change the background to a subtle amber tint and append a dot marker (• View Name (Modified)).
o	Crucial UX Guard: Place the Save Changes and Revert buttons immediately next to the dropdown, flashing them into view with a smooth CSS opacity transition only when isDirty becomes true. This gives clear feedback that the current configuration is in a draft state.
Row 2: The Filter Strip (Inline, Non-Invasive)
•	Avoid the "Collapsible Accordion" Box: Traditional dashboards hide filters inside a heavy drawer or a massive expanding panel that forces the whole table down when opened, causing a frustrating visual jump.
•	Use an Inline Badged Filter Strip: Keep filters resting directly above the table as a sleek row of borderless drop-menus.
•	Visual Pill Indicators: When an option is selected within a dropdown, transform the filter label into a distinct capsule shape containing a clear dismissal option (e.g., [ Stage: Pending ✕ ]). This allows users to check their current filter criteria instantly at a single glance without clicking into anything.
•	The Action Station Area: Group the "Reset" and "Apply" buttons together on the far right of the strip. Keep the "Apply" button styled with your brand's dominant accent color to provide a clear, intuitive endpoint for user interaction.
Row 3: The Data Grid (Maximum Readability)
•	Density Controls: When displaying critical data metrics, some operators prefer a dense spreadsheet view while others prefer roomier, modern rows. Ensure the material-react-table toolbar exposes native density-toggling icons.
•	Frozen Table Header Rows: Pin the table header row directly to the top of the data panel grid block. As users scroll down thousands of data entries, the column labels must remain locked in place so they never lose track of what columns they are evaluating.
•	Micro-Status Visual Tags: Never rely on plain text raw strings to display operational lifecycles like "shipped", "pending", or "cancelled". Wrap them inside rounded status pills featuring high-contrast backgrounds and matching left-aligned colored dot indicators (• Pending).
